Hi,

I'm running Rstudio and shiny server on the same EC2 instance (using Louis Aslett's AMI <http://www.louisaslett.com/RStudio_AMI/>). ( R3.3.1) (RStudio 0.99.903).

It's my first time setting up the configuration of the shiny server, and I have read through the administrator's guide. I'm nearly there, just a step missing.

At EC2, I set up the following ports:

- SShH on port 22;

- HTTP on port 80;

- Custom TCP at 3838;

- Custom TCP at 8787.

I created DNS records linking the elastic IP to bahrainparks.org <http://bahrainparks.org>

When I reach out to the url:

http://mydomain.com/user/AppFolder, I get my application up and running just fine.

However, at http://mydomain.com, I get the Rstudio login.

What I'm looking for is to have the opposite or at least the first requirement:

- get the application at: http://mydomain.com (must!)

- and Rstudio at http://mydomain.com/rstudio (optional)

I have tried dozens of edits to shiny-server.conf, but don't seem to be able to get it right.

To be clear, I don't mind that the application runs on http://mydomain.com/user/AppFolder, as long as this is called when http://mydomain.com <http://mydomain.com/user/AppFolder> is targeted. I have confirmed with AWS that I cannot use DNS to direct to IP/user/folder.

My shiny-server.conf:

run_as shiny;

server {

  listen 3838 127.0.0.1;

  run_as :HOME_USER:;

  location / {

   user_dirs;

  }

}

I have tried, for example, adding the following line to location / :

    app_dir /home/user/AppFolder;

Or, adding a virtual host by adding:

   server_name mydomain.com <http://mydomain.com>;

and the following to / location:

  site_dir /home/user/AppFolder;

I have also tried to send RStudio to another port by adding line:

"www-port=8787" or (3838) to  /etc/rstudio/rserver.conf

But neither have the desired effect. Actually, the only effect this has is to give 502 Bad Gateway when set up to port 3838, even if I have shiny listen to port 80, so there shouldn't be an IP/door clash.

Any hints as to what I'm doing wrong?

According to the administrator's guide, it seems to me that what I need is a virtual host, but then again, I guess I'm not understanding the guide... or I would have made it work by now :)

Many thanks

Joao

